===Etymology 2===
{{wikipedia|Lede paragraph}}

Mid-20th century neologism from a deliberate misspelling of {{m|en|lead}}, intended to avoid confusion with its [[homograph]] meaning a strip of type metal used for positioning type in the frame.<ref name=wotd>WOTD 2000</ref> Compare {{m|en|hed||headline}}.

====Alternative forms====
* [[lead]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{label|en|chiefly US|journalism}} The [[introduction|introductory]] [[paragraph|paragraph(s)]] of a [[newspaper]] or other news [[article]].

=====Quotations=====
* {{seeCites}}

=====Usage notes=====
Usage seems mostly confined to the U.S.<ref>Current citations in Wiktionary, listed [[Citations:lede#English|here]], are from US sources. The only occurrence found in 2008 on ''The Guardian'' website is made by the “editor of Guardian America”, saying “The lede (as we spell it) story in today’s NYT is ...” on his [http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/michaeltomasky/2008/oct/15/johnmccain-williamtimmons op/ed blog]. Other occurrences on .co.uk sites all quote the lead/lede Wikipedia articles.</ref> Originally only journalistic usage that is now so common in general US English that it is no longer labeled as jargon by major US dictionaries such as Merriam-Webster<ref>[http://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/lede ''Lede'' in Merriam-Webster Online]</ref> and American Heritage.<ref>[http://www.thefreedictionary.com/lede ''Lede'' in the American Heritage Dictionary]</ref> Noted as “sometimes spelled” in 1959, “often spelled” in 1969, and asserted in the 1979 reprint of a 1974 book (see [[Citations:lede#English|Citations page]]). In 1990, William Safire was still able to say that "lede" was jargon not listed in regular dictionaries.<ref name=Safire>Safire 1990: "You will not find this spelling in dictionaries; it is still an insiders' variant, steadily growing in frequency of use. [...] Will ''lede'' break out of its insider status and find its way into general use? [...] To suggest this is becoming standard would be misledeing<!--sic:misledeing-->"</ref><ref name=wotd/>

